  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,0,0)$ is $p=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i+\\,j))\\times k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (T(1)\\times N(1))=i-j=⟨1,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+0\\cdot z & =1 \\\\ x-y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
